# Framework git workflow (issue-linked)

## Stranger / cold-clone default

A fresh clone with blank `project.env` (or no file yet) is ordinary work:
edit the open branch in the open folder, create or clone product addons under
ignored `addons/<name>/`, and ignore Jira/worktree ceremony. Fill
`project.env` only for preferences you want (see table below).

## `project.env` preferences

Read gitignored `project.env` (from `docs/examples/root/project.env.example` via
`scripts/workspace/init_workspace.py`). Relevant keys:

| Key | When set | When blank / missing |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WORKTREE_DIR` | Emergency full-clone parent, used only after `git worktree add` fails | No clone fallback — edit the open branch |
| `DEFAULT_JIRA_PROJECT` | Default project key for authorized Jira creates | Do not invent a project; only use a key the operator/context already named |
| `DEFAULT_JIRA_BOARD_ID` | Board id for backlog ↔ board moves | Do not invent a board id |
| `DEFAULT_JIRA_SITE_URL` | Origin for browse links in PR bodies | Omit invented browse URLs |
| `DEFAULT_JIRA_EPIC_KEY` | Default parent Epic key on authorized creates | Do not invent an epic |
| `DEFAULT_JIRA_ASSIGNEE` | Assignee on authorized Jira creates | Leave unassigned unless the operator named someone |
| `DEFAULT_GITHUB_PR_ASSIGNEE` | GitHub login or `@me` for PR assignees | Fall back to `@me` / PR author when blank |

Shell exports of the same names override the file. Helpers:
`scripts/workspace/project_env.py`, `scripts/worktree/bootstrap_worktree_tests.py`,
`scripts/worktree/live_refresh.py`, `scripts/worktree/check_addon_worktree_collisions.py`.

## Default: current branch (no worktree ceremony)

If this turn has **no** issue signal — the user did not name an existing
issue key, did not ask to create-and-implement, and did not name a key to
work — this is ordinary work:

- Stay on the **current branch** / open checkout (do not invent an issue key,
  cut a feature branch for ceremony, or stand up a worktree).
- Do not create a tracker issue to unlock issue-keyed branching or worktrees.
- Commit / push / PR still only when the user asks.

**Create-only is not an issue-work signal.** “Make a Jira / file a Story”
without an explicit implement/own/fix second verb creates the ticket and
stops (`framework-jira-issues`). Do not open a worktree or edit code for that
turn.

Do **not** infer a ticket from topic similarity, prior chats, or “this could
be a Story.” No signal → no worktree force.

## When issue-keyed isolation applies

Apply this section only when **both** are true:

1. An **explicit** issue signal (named key to work/pull/own, or create-and-
   implement in the same session), and
2. The user did not say **on main**, **on live**, **in the open checkout**,
   or **skip the worktree**.

`WORKTREE_DIR` is not a gate. Follow `code-worktrees`:

1. Record the open checkout path. Call it the live checkout for the rest of
   the run.
2. `git worktree add -b <ISSUE-KEY>` at
   `~/.cursor/worktrees/<repo-directory-name>/<ISSUE-KEY>`, based on
   `origin/main`. `<repo-directory-name>` is the basename of the live
   toplevel. Reuse that path when `git worktree list` already has it.
3. Do not call `move_agent_to_root`. If the session root moves there on its
   own, continue there. If it stays, edit that path with absolute paths and
   Shell `working_directory`.
4. Copy operator env with `scripts/worktree/copy_worktree_env.py`
   `--live-root` / `--worktree-root`.
5. Do not edit, branch, or commit issue work in the live checkout. Do not
   mid-run `docker compose` restart or live `git pull`.
6. Touch live only after every required merge:
   `scripts/worktree/live_refresh.py --live-root <recorded-live-path>`.

**Creation failed** only when `git worktree add` fails. Then:

- Non-blank `WORKTREE_DIR`: use the clone layout below.
- Blank `WORKTREE_DIR`: edit the open branch in the open folder. Say that
  in one sentence.

Ticket create authorization stays in `framework-jira-issues`. A missing
execute paste does not authorize edits in the live checkout when an issue
signal is present — unless they said on main / on live / skip the worktree.

## Emergency clone layout

Use this only after `git worktree add` failed and `WORKTREE_DIR` is set.
Portable habits stay in `code-worktrees`.

0. Reuse `$WORKTREE_DIR/<ISSUE-KEY>` when it already exists for this issue.
1. Clone current `origin/main` into `$WORKTREE_DIR/<ISSUE-KEY>`. For each
   touched custom addon, clone that remote into
   `$WORKTREE_DIR/<ISSUE-KEY>-<name>`.
2. Do all inspect / edit / test / commit / push / PR work under those paths.
3. Do not call `move_agent_to_root` into the clone.
4. Same live-refresh rule as above. Before branching an addon clone, run
   `scripts/worktree/check_addon_worktree_collisions.py`.

## Live / main escape hatch

If the user explicitly says to do the work **on main**, **on live**, **in
the open checkout**, or **skip the worktree**, follow that for this run even
when an issue key is in play. Prefer the smallest live touch that matches
the ask; do not invent a worktree against that instruction.

## Unexpected code — investigate before discard

Agents often misread **committed** work on `main` / live as “dirty worktree
noise” and run `git restore`, `git checkout --`, `git reset`, or silent
reverts. That destroys real landings from merges, other sessions, or
commits this chat has not seen.

**Before any discard/revert/clean of surprising paths:**

1. Confirm **which checkout** you are looking at (live open root, the
   `~/.cursor/worktrees/<repo>/<ISSUE-KEY>` tree, or a `WORKTREE_DIR`
   fallback clone). Do not call a worktree dirty from live `git status`,
   or the reverse.
2. Run `git status` in that checkout. Split **uncommitted** dirt from
   **already-committed** tree content.
3. If the tree is clean (or the surprise is already in `HEAD`) and the
   content still looks wrong for this session: inspect history first —
   `git log --oneline -20`, and for the path
   `git log --oneline -10 -- <path>` (and `git show` / blame when needed).
   Prefer explaining “this came from commit …” over reverting it.
4. `git fetch` and compare to `origin/main` when the surprise may be a
   pull/merge you have not absorbed yet. Do not treat “I did not author
   this file in this chat” as proof it is junk.

**Hard refuse by default:** mass restore, hard reset, or reverting
unexpected paths on `main` / live solely because they were not in your
mental model of the session. Discard only when the operator explicitly
asks, or when you can prove the change is **uncommitted local dirt you
created this session** and undoing it is the task.

## Branching

1. Branch off `main` **inside the isolated checkout** (update/rebase when
   practical). `git worktree add -b` already does this for the primary path.
2. Name the branch exactly the issue key the operator named.
3. No alternate slugs or ticket-less feature branches for issue-linked work.
4. One issue → one working branch name unless the user splits the work.

## Custom addon remotes

Cloned custom addons live under ignored `addons/<name>/` (each its own remote).
Any implementation edit there is in scope for the same git treatment:

1. **Required when touched:** same issue-keyed branch, commit(s), push, and PR
   on that addon remote when the user asks to land work. Framework-only edits
   need no addon branch/PR. Create the addon checkout with `git worktree add`
   in that addon repo at
   `~/.cursor/worktrees/<addon-directory-name>/<ISSUE-KEY>`. On the clone
   fallback, use `$WORKTREE_DIR/<ISSUE-KEY>-<name>` instead. Do not edit the
   live `addons/<name>/` checkout.
2. **Align in flight:** identical issue-key branch name on framework and every
   touched addon. Branch each addon from its integration branch (usually
   `main`, or whichever branch carries that checkout's addon tree).
3. **No orphan dirty end state:** do not leave issue-owned addon changes only on
   disk; land every touched remote together when asked.
4. **Isolation:** say `addons/<name>/` only — no specific product-addon
   identities in tracked framework policy (`framework-custom-addon-isolation`).
5. Before branching an addon checkout, run
   `scripts/worktree/check_addon_worktree_collisions.py`. A skip because
   `WORKTREE_DIR` is unset is fine on the `git worktree` path.

## Worktree pytest + GUI vitest

Use `scripts/worktree/bootstrap_worktree_tests.py` with the checkout you
actually created (`~/.cursor/worktrees/...` or the `WORKTREE_DIR` clone):

1. Keep the addon git checkout separate from the framework worktree.
2. Bootstrap rsyncs the addon into `<worktree>/addons/<name>/` as a **real
   directory** (never symlink the clone into the worktree `addons/` tree —
   pytest resolves symlinks and SQLAlchemy double-registers).
3. Bootstrap ensures `addons/__init__.py` and symlinks GUI `node_modules` from
   the live checkout into the worktree GUI runtime.
4. Run pytest from a cwd **outside** the live checkout with `PYTHONPATH` and
   `FRAMEWORK_INFRASTRUCTURE__CUSTOM_ADDONS_DIR` pointing at the worktree,
   plus `--import-mode=importlib`.
5. Run vitest from `<worktree>/framework/runtimes/gui`.
6. Hard-fail if asked to stage tests against the live checkout or live
   `addons/*` (no rsync/checkout/clean against live for tests).

Optional execute pastes: `docs/prompts/jira_execute.md`,
`docs/prompts/jira_execute_auto.md` (only when this checkout uses Jira).

## Pull requests

When asked to open a PR (framework and/or touched addon remotes):

1. **Title:** Conventional Commits (imperative, optional scope, no trailing
   period), same shape as `global-commit-messages`.
2. **Body:** summary-only — what/why bullets plus a tracker browse link when
   `DEFAULT_JIRA_SITE_URL` is set and the work is issue-linked. No Test plan
   unless the user asks this turn. Omit the link when the site URL is unset.
3. **Assignee:** `DEFAULT_GITHUB_PR_ASSIGNEE` from `project.env` when set
   (e.g. `@me` or a GitHub login); otherwise assign the PR author / `@me`
   through the ready GitHub MCP when present (`global-mcp-first`). Do not use
   `gh` for assignee or other PR ops while that MCP is ready.
4. Open and merge addon PR(s) alongside the framework PR when addons were
   touched; the same user ask covers every changed remote. Use a ready
   GitHub MCP for create/merge/checks (`global-mcp-first`).

## Status handoff

Only for Jira-linked work when this checkout uses Jira: follow
`framework-jira-status` (board id from `DEFAULT_JIRA_BOARD_ID` when set).
`Done` only after every required PR has merged (framework + touched addons),
or the Task finishes with no PR.
